{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Gerrit LLM-Review +1 (PASS) on change 1708 with 3 advisory findings, all marked resolved and non-blocking. Recording them here rather than amending, since amending would reset the +1, re-run CI, and widen the window for a baseline-file rebase against sibling b2bb for no behavioral gain:\n\n1. (maintainability, _blockers.py) The guard 'relation not in _BLOCKING_RELATIONS or relation != \"depends_on\"' is redundant — depends_on is a member of the frozenset, so the first clause is dead. Accurate. It is a faithful carry-over of the original's nested 'if relation in _BLOCKING_RELATIONS: if relation == \"depends_on\"', preserved deliberately in a behavior-preserving refactor and explained in the new docstring. Removing it would also strand _status.py's _BLOCKING_RELATIONS re-export, which exists solely for _blockers and which this ticket scopes as untouched; other users (_hierarchy, doctor) import the constant straight from _relations, so the tidy fix is to retire that indirection in its own change.\n\n2. (tests, _graph.py) check_cycle_at_level's target-side level guard (_is_at_level refusing to traverse into a neighbour of a different ticket_type) is untested — every existing test uses one uniform level. True, and PRE-EXISTING: the same guard was inline in the original loop and equally uncovered. The extraction did not change it.\n\n3. (tests, _unblock.py) _load_states_with_tombstones (stub-yielding when reduce returns None but a tombstone exists, tombstone-status override, malformed-tombstone -> 'deleted') has no test driving it through detect_newly_unblocked. Also pre-existing: that logic was inline in detect_newly_unblocked before this change with the same coverage.\n\nFindings 2 and 3 are genuine coverage gaps in the graph subsystem that this refactor surfaced by naming the seams, but did not introduce. They are worth a follow-up ticket under the ae1b remediation epic rather than scope creep here."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786602676984686001,"uuid":"29465c96-6aee-429f-942f-c0f519a22c0e"}